# SUM(1)

## NAME

sum - sum and count blocks in a file

## SYNOPSIS

| **sum** [ file ... ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Sum* calculates and prints a 16-bit checksum for the named files, or for the standard input if
no name appears, and also prints the number of blocks in each. It is typically used to look for
bad spots, or to validate a file communicated over some transmission line.

**Note:** the checksum is **still 16 bits** and is bit for bit the one a PDP-11 computed for
the same file, although a word on this machine is 48. The algorithm rotates a sixteen-bit
accumulator right, adds the byte and masks back to sixteen bits, and because the mask is
applied on every byte rather than left to the register's width, a wider machine cannot diverge
from a narrower one.

**Note:** the file name is printed only when **more than one** file was named. A single named
file gets a checksum and a block count and nothing else, which is v7's test - **argc > 2,** not
**argc > 1.**

## BLOCKS ARE 1024 BYTES

**Note:** the block *sum* counts in is **1024** bytes. It is not this machine's filesystem
block, which is **3072** bytes - 512 words of 48 bits - and is three of them.

So every number *sum* prints is a **multiple of three,** the smallest thing that can be
allocated being one 3072-byte block: a one-byte file reads as three. And the numbers are
**twice** a PDP-11's, v7 having counted in 512-byte blocks.

Du(1), *df*(1M), *quot*(1M) and ls(1) report in the same block.

## DIAGNOSTICS

`Read error` is indistinguishable from end of file on most devices; check the block count.

A file that cannot be opened adds 10 to the exit status and a read error adds 1, so a run that
failed to open one file exits 10. That is v7's arithmetic, unchanged.

## SEE ALSO

wc(1), cmp(1), du(1)
